# The only defect in this lane that is not a wrong parent, and the reason the
# instrument that reported it needed adjudicating.
#
# `rack` charges toml 29 bytes and files them as a derivation dispute. They are
# not. Both parsers hang the comment under the SAME parent; outliner just
# forgets to grow the parent over it.
#
#   outliner                      tree-sitter
#   pair [0, 7)     <- 7           pair [0, 12)   <- 12
#     bare_key [0, 1)                bare_key [0, 1)
#     string [4, 7)                  string [4, 7)
#     comment [9, 12)                comment [9, 12)
#
# Read the extents. `comment [9, 12)` is a child of `pair [0, 7)` - a child
# that begins two bytes after its parent ends. `tool/standing.py` has been
# saying so all along, on the same grammar it scores 100.0% standing:
#
#   sound  1 of 30 UNSOUND - toml: 1 loose, 0 disorder, 0 torn
#     [child outside its parent: comment [47, 56) in pair [27, 45)]
#
# Nobody chose a different tree here. Every name and every nesting agrees. The
# defect is one number on one node, and it is a worse claim than a misplaced
# comment: a tree with a child outside its parent is not a tree, and anything
# that walks it by extent - which is what `rack`'s own `cover()` does, and says
# so in its docstring - is walking a shape that cannot exist.
#
# 18 of the 29 charged bytes are `v`, `=` and `"1"`, whose parent both parsers
# agree on. They are charged because their PARENT's right edge moved.
roots 1
mends 0
holds pair
holds comment
spans pair 0 12
spans comment 9 12
